        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 120,
        "headingId": 374,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-14T11:38:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 462,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 375,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62620,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 120,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ovary</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-14T11:38:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62621,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 120,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Pregnancy luteoma is usually an incidental finding near term, during caesarean section, or immediately postpartum in patients 15&ndash;44&nbsp;years of age {24335664}. Rare androgenic manifestations&nbsp;&ndash; maternal and/or fetal&nbsp;&ndash; have been reported {24126228; 17915067; 11870143; 24335664}. Ultrasound and MRI show a predominantly solid, heterogeneous mass with prominent vascularity, which may mimic ovarian neoplasia {19243065}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-14T11:38:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62622,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 120,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>This is a rare lesion, but it has been reported to be more common in multiparous and African-American patients {4290238}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-14T11:38:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62623,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 120,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>A potential role of hCG has been postulated.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-14T11:38:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62624,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 120,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Pregnancy luteoma may evolve from nodular hyperplasia of theca interna cells, but the precise pathogenesis is unknown.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-14T11:38:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62625,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 120,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Pregnancy luteoma is most often unilateral, rarely bilateral. The size ranges from 1.5&nbsp;to &gt;&nbsp;20&nbsp;cm and the cut surface is multinodular, soft, and brown and may show focal haemorrhage {24335664}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-14T11:38:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62626,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 120,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Histopathology reveals a multinodular proliferation of luteinized cells with round nuclei and abundant eosinophilic cytoplasm. Follicle-like spaces are common and may contain eosinophilic secretions. Occasional prominent nucleoli and increased mitotic activity may be seen. The cytoplasm may contain eosinophilic globules. Reinke crystals are absent. Lesions removed postpartum typically show regressive changes. Reticulin staining shows fibres around groups of cells. The differential diagnosis includes corpus luteum of pregnancy and steroid cell tumour NOS.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-14T11:38:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62627,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 120,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-14T11:38:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62628,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 120,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-14T11:38:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 472,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 385,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62629,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 120,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> occurrence during late pregnancy or postpartum; characteristic gross appearance and hyperplastic proliferation of large luteinized ovarian cells on microscopy.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-14T11:38:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 470,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 383,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62630,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 120,
        "headingId": 383,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-14T11:38:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 471,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 384,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62631,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 120,
        "headingId": 384,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These are benign lesions that usually regress spontaneously postpartum.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-14T11:38:23"
      }
    ],
